# Sources for the February 1949 attempt, martial law, Tudeh ban, and
# constitutional revision: Habib Ladjevardi, Labor Unions and Autocracy in
# Iran, pp. 90–92, for the exact 4 February date and immediate suppression;
# Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5, pp. 249–250;
# Mostafa Elm, Oil, Power, and Principle, ch. 3, “The Constitution.”
# First-Senate composition: official First-Senate roster, appointed and
# elected tables. Choice effects are counterfactual abstractions.
title: The attempt on the <span class="term term-royalist">Shah</span>
subtitle: An attack becomes a constitutional emergency
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1949 and month >= 2 and attempt_emergency_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 30
on-arrival: attempt_emergency_seen = 1

= 4 February 1949

An assailant fires on {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Mohammad Reza
Shah{!</span>!} and wounds him. Before the opposition can establish who knew
what, the attack has become the justification for an emergency. The government
declares martial law, begins arrests, and prepares measures against its
political opponents.

The attack is documented; a larger directing conspiracy is not established.
The emerging opposition must decide what standard to use when an allegation
is useful, frightening, and still unproved.

- @attempt_caution
- @attempt_quiet
- @attempt_joint

@attempt_caution
title: Insist that evidence precede accusation
subtitle: The statement sets a public rule without committing the opposition to a campaign it cannot yet sustain
on-arrival: attempt_response = "Evidence before accusation"; constitutional_legitimacy += 2

Your statement condemns the shooting and refuses to assign a wider
responsibility without evidence. It gives opponents of collective punishment
a public standard they can repeat, even though it does little to reveal what
the authorities intend to do next.

The emergency does not wait for that evidence. Arrests and restrictions are
already widening.

- @post_event: Continue

@attempt_quiet
title: Gather reports before intervening publicly
subtitle: A documented case can be assembled before the opposition exposes itself in print
on-arrival: attempt_response = "Quiet fact gathering"; constitutional_legitimacy += 1; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 2

Lawyers, editors, and political contacts compare what they have heard and mark
contradictions rather than forcing them into one story. The resulting record
separates verified acts from politically convenient inferences.

Because the work is discreet, the opposition establishes less of a public
standard before emergency measures take effect.

- @post_event: Continue

@attempt_joint
title: Draft a joint statement for opposition newspapers
subtitle: A common text brings the opposition into view—and under the <span class="term term-royalist">Court</span>'s eye
on-arrival: attempt_response = "Joint newspaper statement"; constitutional_legitimacy += 1; organizational_reach += 1; shah_resistance += 1

Several opposition circles agree on careful language: condemn the assault,
reject unsupported collective blame, and ask under what law the emergency
will be administered. Drafting together creates a modest working connection
that did not exist before the crisis.

It also identifies the signatories to a {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} newly alert to organized
opposition. The statement cannot prevent the measures that follow.

- @post_event: Continue

@emergency_measures
title: The <span class="term term-left">Tudeh</span> ban and opposition crackdown
subtitle: Emergency power reaches parties, newspapers, and political networks
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1949 and month >= 2 and attempt_emergency_seen = 1 and emergency_crackdown_seen = 0 and attempt_response != "Not yet reached"
max-visits: 1
priority: 29
on-arrival: emergency_crackdown_seen = 1

= February 1949

[? if attempt_response = "Evidence before accusation": The opposition's first
statement established an evidentiary rule; lawyers now try to apply it while
emergency decrees arrive faster than they can be examined. ?]
[? if attempt_response = "Quiet fact gathering": Reports collected before the
public intervention give lawyers a firmer chronology, but newspapers have not
yet heard a common opposition voice. ?]
[? if attempt_response = "Joint newspaper statement": The joint statement has
made the responding editors and politicians visible to authorities enforcing
the emergency. ?]

Under martial law the state outlaws the {!<span class="term term-left">!}Tudeh
Party{!</span>!}, arrests political opponents, and restricts newspapers and
public activity. Constitutional opposition must now work under these
conditions.

The immediate question is where to put scarce attention. A legal record can
contest the normalization of emergency rule. Discreet contacts can preserve
some organizational knowledge. A public civil-liberties defense can keep the
principle visible but expose its organizers to further pressure.

- @ban_legal_record
- @ban_contacts
- @ban_public_defense

@ban_legal_record
title: Build a legal record of the emergency measures
subtitle: The emergency decrees become a future constitutional brief rather than an immediate street contest
on-arrival: emergency_response = "Legal record"; constitutional_legitimacy += 2; shah_relation -= 1

Lawyers begin recording orders, detentions, closures, and the authority
claimed for them. The record cannot reopen a newspaper or free a detainee by
itself, but it prevents every measure from dissolving into rumor.

The choice also makes the opposition's constitutional objection harder for
the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} to treat as private unease.

- @post_event: Continue

@ban_contacts
title: Preserve discreet organizational contacts
subtitle: Political networks survive quietly while the public defense is left to others
on-arrival: emergency_response = "Discreet contacts"; organizational_reach += 2

Trusted intermediaries carry news between lawyers, political circles, and
families affected by the arrests. The network learns which channels still
function and which reports can be corroborated.

The work preserves capacity for a later campaign, but people facing the
emergency hear no comparably strong common public answer.

- @post_event: Continue

@ban_public_defense
title: Coordinate a public defense of political liberties
subtitle: The ban is answered in print and meeting halls, where the <span class="term term-royalist">Crown</span> cannot ignore it
on-arrival: emergency_response = "Public liberties campaign"; public_mandate += 1; press_capacity += 1; shah_resistance += 1

Editors and constitutional politicians frame the ban and press restrictions
as a test of lawful government rather than as proof of an unverified
conspiracy. The argument gives later opposition writing a common vocabulary.

Publication under martial law is uneven, and the visible coordination gives
the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} another reason to regard these circles as a developing challenge.

- @post_event: Continue

@constituent_assembly
title: The constituent assembly
subtitle: Constitutional revision changes the balance between <span class="term term-royalist">Crown</span> and <span class="term term-parliament">Majles</span>
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1949 and month >= 5
max-visits: 1
priority: 20
on-arrival: constituent_assembly_seen = 1

= Spring 1949

[? if emergency_response = "Legal record": The legal archive from the
emergency gives constitutional opponents a record of how exceptional power
has already been used. ?]
[? if emergency_response = "Discreet contacts": Preserved contacts can carry
instructions, but they cannot substitute for a public constitutional
position. ?]
[? if emergency_response = "Public liberties campaign": The civil-liberties
campaign gives the opposition a public language and gives the
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} another reason to
expect organized resistance. ?]

The constituent assembly strengthens the constitutional position of
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Mohammad Reza Shah{!</span>!}. Among the
changes, revised Article 48 gives the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Shah{!</span>!} a power to dissolve the
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}.

The practical disagreement is how to answer it. One course preserves a formal
objection to the way constitutional power is being altered. Another treats
the revision as an operating risk and prepares a procedural memorandum for
future deputies. A third begins building a dissolution-response network.
Before the elections, the loose opposition has enough shared lawyers and
organizers to make only one of these a common priority.

- @assembly_procedure
- @assembly_risk
- @assembly_organize

@assembly_procedure
title: Publish a signed constitutional objection
subtitle: A public roll of signatures turns constitutional objection into an open challenge
on-arrival: assembly_response = "Signed constitutional objection"; constitutional_legitimacy += 1; shah_resistance += 2

The opposition records that constitutional forms do not make every
constitutional change legitimate. Its objection gives later parliamentary
arguments a clear lineage and makes accommodation harder to misrepresent as
consent.

The {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!}, in turn, has little difficulty identifying who intends to contest
the expanded royal power.

- @post_event: Continue

@assembly_risk
title: Prepare a procedural memorandum for future deputies
subtitle: Future deputies receive a case they can use inside the chamber; the public dispute remains muted
on-arrival: assembly_response = "Procedural memorandum"; constitutional_legitimacy += 1; parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy += 2

Attention shifts to procedure, possible triggers for dissolution, and the
people through whom royal influence might reach an election. The opposition
has a procedural answer ready when pressure develops.

That preparation is useful but quiet. The public record contains a less
forceful common objection to the revision itself.

- @post_event: Continue

@assembly_organize
title: Build a dissolution-response network
subtitle: Local allies prepare for dissolution without making legal doctrine their common language
on-arrival: assembly_response = "Dissolution-response network"; organizational_reach += 1; shah_resistance += 1

The opposition assigns organizers to keep contacts current and to prepare a
common response if dissolution pressure interrupts parliamentary work. The
network creates no power to reverse Article 48 and makes no prediction that
dissolution must occur.

Choosing organization leaves fewer shared lawyers for a detailed memorandum or
formal public objection. It also makes the opposition's preparation more
visible to the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!}.

- @post_event: Continue

@senate_election_preparations
title: A new <span class="term term-senate">Senate</span> and a disputed election
subtitle: Two chambers, mixed routes to office, and no common opposition yet
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1949 and month >= 7
max-visits: 1
priority: 20
on-arrival: parliamentary_deck_unlocked = 1; majles_phase = "Sixteenth Majles election under way"

= Summer–Autumn 1949

[? if assembly_response = "Signed constitutional objection": Signatories to
the spring objection enter the election with a public record and with the
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} already attentive to
their cooperation. ?]
[? if assembly_response = "Procedural memorandum": The memorandum supplies
candidates with a common constitutional argument but little local machinery
for recording interference. ?]
[? if assembly_response = "Dissolution-response network": The contacts built
against dissolution can now collect election reports, though their earlier
purpose has made them conspicuous. ?]

Preparations for the first {!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!}
coincide with elections for the Sixteenth
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}. The {!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!} will
combine elected and royally appointed members. In the {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} contest,
complaints about interference remain scattered among candidates, newspapers,
and local political circles.

No opposition organization commands the others. The immediate task is
narrower: create enough common method that separate complaints can become a
defensible constitutional campaign.

- @senate_committee
- @senate_constitution
- @senate_correspondents

@senate_committee
title: Prepare a common election-monitoring committee
subtitle: Comparable reports matter more than a single dramatic denunciation
on-arrival: election_preparation_response = "Election-monitoring committee"; organizational_reach += 1

Representatives agree on a simple intake: where voting occurred, who reports
interference, and what evidence can be preserved. The committee has no power
to certify a national result, but it gives otherwise isolated candidates a
place to compare cases.

When the Tehran dispute sharpens, a small coordinating apparatus is ready to
carry the complaints toward a common protest.

- @post_event: Continue

@senate_constitution
title: Prepare a public constitutional appeal
subtitle: The new chamber is opposed before the public, with fewer organizers left to watch its making
on-arrival: election_preparation_response = "Public constitutional appeal"; public_mandate += 1

The appeal connects representative elections to constitutional government
without pretending that every disputed return has already been proved. It
gives the opposition a public principle around which different politicians
can gather.

The case is legible, but the underlying monitoring record remains uneven.

- @post_event: Continue

@senate_correspondents
title: Recruit provincial and Tehran correspondents
subtitle: A dispersed correspondence network sacrifices a single line for wider provincial participation
on-arrival: election_preparation_response = "Provincial correspondents"; organizational_reach += 1

Editors, lawyers, and political acquaintances begin passing reports from
constituencies to Tehran. They do not produce a complete national ledger, but
they help the opposition distinguish recurring methods from one unverified
complaint.

The correspondents create a wider reporting network; agreement on a single
public demand will have to be forged in the approaching protest.

- @post_event: Continue
